## Triage Summary

Merged ticket **#4471** contains **427 reports** across **5 distinct problems**. The original desk classification treated this as one issue, but secondary analysis reveals multiple independent bugs affecting date and time handling in `sched-prod`.

## Problems Identified

### 1. Wrong Day of Week on Invite — 111 reports

**Severity**: High  
**Status**: Open ([GitHub #1](https://github.com/OldMetalmind/s01e03-ticket-4471/issues/1))

Calendar invites display incorrect day-of-week names, causing customers to arrive on the wrong day even when the underlying date is correct.

**Example**: Booking for Monday 11 May gets invite saying "Tuesday 12 May". Customer arrives Monday; appointment is missed.

**Affected locales**: en-GB (27), en-US (12), pl-PL (10), nl-NL (9), da-DK (9), and others.

**Root cause**: Day-of-week calculation bug in invite generation or date serialization. May be related to timezone offset or date rounding in the day-name lookup code.

---

### 2. Date Format Parsing Error: DD/MM vs MM/DD Confusion — 132 reports

**Severity**: High  
**Status**: Open ([GitHub #7](https://github.com/OldMetalmind/s01e03-ticket-4471/issues/7))

Dates are parsed or stored with month and day swapped, causing cascading failures across the system. This is the root cause of both:
- Booking mismatches (UI shows one date, invite shows another)
- Locale-specific format reversals

**Examples**:
- "Booking shows 3 October in UI but invite says 10 March" — day and month completely swapped
- "Every booking in June is one day off. Only June." — systematic parsing error for specific date ranges
- Invite says "Tuesday 12 May" when calendar booked for "Monday 11 May"

**Affected locales**: en-GB (15), it-IT (7), de-DE (4), pl-PL (4), fr-FR (4), pt-PT (4), en-US (4), and others.

**Root cause**: Date parsing or serialization logic incorrectly handles locale-specific date formats:
- System expects MM/DD but receives DD/MM (or vice versa)
- Inconsistent format handling between UI layer and backend API
- Date transformation that doesn't account for locale context
- Possible issue with CLDR locale data vs hardcoded format strings

---

### 3. Appointment Auto-Moved Without User Action — 34 reports

**Severity**: Critical  
**Status**: Open ([GitHub #4](https://github.com/OldMetalmind/s01e03-ticket-4471/issues/4))

Appointments automatically change date/time by several months without user action. This is a **data integrity issue** that destroys customer trust.

**Example**: Appointment moves from 7 April to 4 July (3 months forward).

**Root cause**: Unknown. Suspects:
- Background job with incorrect date recalculation logic
- Database trigger with wrong math
- Scheduled task that runs unexpectedly

**Investigation**: Check cron jobs, background workers, and data migration logs from April onwards.

---

### 4. Reminder Sent on Wrong Date — 37 reports

**Severity**: Medium  
**Status**: Open ([GitHub #5](https://github.com/OldMetalmind/s01e03-ticket-4471/issues/5))

Appointment reminders are sent weeks or months before/after the actual appointment.

**Example**: "Customer booked 6 November and got an invite for 11 June."

**Root cause**: Reminder scheduling uses wrong date:
- Timezone mismatch
- Date format parsing error
- Off-by-one or off-by-month calculation

---

### 5. Other Date/Time Issues — 113 reports

**Severity**: Medium  
**Status**: Open ([GitHub #6](https://github.com/OldMetalmind/s01e03-ticket-4471/issues/6))

113 reports (26% of merged ticket) don't fit cleanly into categories above. Likely contain:
- Additional timezone issues
- Locale-specific edge cases
- Recurring patterns not yet identified

**Next step**: Secondary triage required on sample of 20-30 reports to find patterns and potentially split into sub-issues.

---

